      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4 leading-tight">
        BC PNP High Economic Impact Draws: 2026 Thresholds and What They Mean for 2027
      </h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 mb-4">
        Innovate is the only BC PNP objective with no occupation list. You get in either by earning enough or by scoring high enough. Here is every 2026 threshold, how the BC PNP counts your wage, and how to plan for 2027.
      </p>
      <div class="flex items-center gap-4 text-sm text-gray-500">
        <time datetime="2026-10-14">October 14, 2026</time>
        <span>&bull;</span>
        <span>11 min read</span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 mb-10">
      <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6 rounded-2xl text-center">
        <div class="text-3xl font-black text-indigo-600 mb-2">$52/hr</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-slate-600 font-medium">Wage lane, Sept 24, 2026 (plus $105,000/yr)</div>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6 rounded-2xl text-center">
        <div class="text-3xl font-black text-green-600 mb-2">131</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-slate-600 font-medium">Points lane cutoff, Sept 24, 2026</div>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6 rounded-2xl text-center">
        <div class="text-3xl font-black text-orange-600 mb-2">3,974</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-slate-600 font-medium">High Economic Impact invitations in 2026 to date</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">

      <p class="text-xl leading-relaxed">
        When the BC PNP restructured on April 23, 2026, two of its three objectives came with occupation lists: Care (36 occupations) and Build (9 trades). The third, Innovate, did not. WelcomeBC describes it this way: "The BC PNP will continue to conduct High Economic Impact invitations to apply (ITA) that target top talent in all sectors." The selection-of-workers page adds that these invitations target "top talent across all sectors, including experienced entrepreneurs." For most skilled workers who are not in healthcare, childcare, education, veterinary care or the nine trades, High Economic Impact is now the main way into the BC PNP.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">How a High Economic Impact Round Works</h2>

      <p>Every Skills Immigration High Economic Impact round in 2026 has had two separate lanes, published together on the same day:</p>

      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>The wage lane.</strong> Candidates whose job offer met a minimum hourly wage <em>and</em> a minimum annual wage, in an occupation classified as NOC TEER 0, 1, 2 or 3. No minimum score applied; WelcomeBC lists the score as "N/A."</li>
        <li><strong>The points lane.</strong> Candidates with a registration score at or above a cutoff, regardless of wage or occupation (subject to the normal eligibility rules).</li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        Section 7.3(a) of the Skills Immigration Program Guide lists the factors the BC PNP may use to rank and target invitations: education, B.C. professional designation, language, occupation, work experience, "wage and/or skill level of job offer," intent to settle in a region, and "strategic priorities." The invitations page warns after each round that "future high economic impact invitations to apply may be based on different factors." The two-lane structure is a practice, not a rule.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Every High Economic Impact Round in 2026</h2>

      <p>All figures below are from WelcomeBC's invitations page. The rounds were labelled "High Economic Impact" in February and April and "Innovate: High Economic Impact" from May onward.</p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden text-sm">
